## How do you become an SME in a new system?
"I start by reading existing documentation and code — understanding what exists before suggesting changes. Then I trace one full request flow end-to-end — from API call to DB and back. I ask questions in team discussions to fill gaps. Within 2–3 sprints, I take ownership of one module — I fix bugs, write improvements, review PRs for that area. SME status comes from consistent contribution, not just reading."
--------------------------------------------------------------------
## Contributing when you're not the most senior?
"I come prepared to meetings — I read the ticket or design doc beforehand. If I have a question or concern, I raise it with a concrete example, not just 'I'm not sure about this.' I ask 'have we considered X?' rather than 'X is wrong.' Senior people appreciate a junior who adds signal, not noise. I also follow up after meetings — if something was unclear, I clarify async instead of letting ambiguity carry into development."
--------------------------------------------------------------------
## How do you collaborate with QA?
"I share the API contract before I start coding — QA writes test cases in parallel. I flag edge cases I'm aware of — they might not think of IVR-specific scenarios like duplicate calls or timeout retries. When QA finds a bug, I don't debate it — I reproduce it first, then fix or explain. After fixing, I tell them exactly what changed so they know what to retest. Good developer-QA relationship = fewer production bugs."
--------------------------------------------------------------------
## How do you document a microservice?
"Four things: 
(1) README — what this service does, how to run it locally. 
(2) API contract — endpoints, request/response, error codes. 
(3) Architecture decision records — why we made key design choices (so future developers don't undo them without context). 
--------------------------------------------------------------------
## Translating business requirements to technical design?
"I listen for the business outcome first — not the feature request. If business says 'customers are calling back because they don't know their claim status,' the technical solution might be a notification service, not just an API. I ask: what triggers this? Who uses it? What's the failure scenario? Then I write a one-page technical proposal with the approach and trade-offs. I get sign-off before writing code — not after."
